import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t as i,P as n,q as a}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var r=i("<h1>Oceans & Marine Governance Framework v2.3.1</h1> <h2>Ocean Rights, Indigenous and Coastal Stewardship, Regenerative Blue Economy, Precaution, and Constitutionally Bounded Marine Authority</h2> <p><strong>Framework ID:</strong> <code>framework_oceans_marine_governance</code><br> <strong>Document type:</strong> Tier 2 Foundational Application Layer — Core Governance Document<br> <strong>Version:</strong> 2.3.1<br> <strong>Date:</strong> 2026-08-01<br> <strong>Status:</strong> Controlled constitutional successor — cluster-conformant patch <strong>Conforms to:</strong> <code>CERGTA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Supersedes:</strong> Oceans & Marine Governance Framework v2.2<br> <strong>Authority effect:</strong> None until lawfully adopted in a jurisdiction</p> <hr> <h2>0. Controlled successor notice</h2> <p>Oceans v2.3 preserves the source framework’s animating vision: the ocean as the Blue Heart of the Planet; Indigenous and coastal stewardship; legal rights for marine ecosystems; precaution toward deep-sea mining, geoengineering, and biotechnology; just transition for fishers; regenerative finance; quieter shipping; marine restoration; and real consequences for destructive practices.</p> <p>It replaces the v2.2 assumption that an MGCC vote, BAZ designation, data-twin alert, acoustic log, ISA permit, blacklist tier, future date, coalition, or ecological threshold can move directly into quota, tariff, port denial, licence revocation, seizure, emergency deployment, or punishment.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Marine urgency does not create marine jurisdiction.</strong></p></blockquote> <blockquote><p><strong>Observation is not attribution. A model is not a quota. A quota is not a licence. A tariff is not a blacklist. A listing is not port denial. Port denial is not seizure. Personhood is not title. Precaution is not permanent prohibition. Emergency need is not command.</strong></p></blockquote> <p>The framework may coordinate, observe, advise, develop model standards, support affected Indigenous nations and coastal communities, facilitate restoration and just transition, administer programmes or funds where separately chartered, and prepare lawful records. It cannot create a global ocean sovereign, grant personhood, authenticate sacredness, issue universal fishing licences or port orders, adjudicate guilt, seize vessels, deny humanitarian assistance, declare emergencies, or command force.</p> <h2>1. Source freeze and controlling instruments</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>File</th><th>SHA-256</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oceans & Marine Governance Framework v2.2</td><td>oceans-marine-governance-framework-v2.2.md</td><td><code>958a401c4b3465bfae7f9dd1bb305dbf35790b303300daab7d4f37afa65e3c72</code></td></tr><tr><td>Commons/Ecological Authority Audit v0.1</td><td>commons-ecological-authority-cluster-constitutional-audit-v0.1.md</td><td><code>974dfd8dd4bf5b623bd5e7e544b7afdbbd89869b66f107db293cfe6af67a043b</code></td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td>commons-ecological-rights-guardianship-title-resource-authority-interface-specification-v0.1.md</td><td><code>3c45e37c834c727471657fc91149edbe3011137562c2dd73e54b20226c92589b</code></td></tr><tr><td>EIOI v2.7</td><td>ecological-intelligence-observation-interpretation-layer-v2.7.md</td><td><code>41cb7e607e8c3f8776ca74f3ba46ab0ff6bcce94fc24b35178fc4cd94d80d9a2</code></td></tr><tr><td>Planetary Health v0.9</td><td>planetary-health-governance-framework-v0.9.md</td><td><code>259083f6039180b4532ae168ff441245e526570c6442371814cbb238e669dc13</code></td></tr><tr><td>Biodiversity v1.1</td><td>biodiversity-implementation-framework-v1.1.md</td><td><code>8f29fa32ac4b76354030b55c61765a77e8c558d64e19627108d7c254d433ec1a</code></td></tr><tr><td>Water & Sanitation v5.2</td><td>water-sanitation-governance-framework-v5.2.md</td><td><code>3612de56de57d1940b278a7e7269588fc08cd964fd97bc33b8ebe8f7d7d69b97</code></td></tr><tr><td>Indigenous Sovereignty & Planetary Healing v1.1</td><td>indigenous-sovereignty-planetary-healing-v1.1.md</td><td><code>2e62ba2c00048ce20e4c9df7ffb9ce0338e358ee866e515bf102f4cc4e49df22</code></td></tr><tr><td>Justice Systems v1.4</td><td>justice-systems-framework-v1.4.md</td><td><code>08a8fdc78d6cd3928178cb5d98fdf8325b1fd4c9f016bb0c872e6b56b87d3ee4</code></td></tr><tr><td>ECRC/0.1</td><td>emergency-catastrophic-risk-continuity-interface-specification-v0.1.md</td><td><code>c673742b1317c7169d4b8226118478c990e9a3d05084b2dc5ac9ad2f1971163c</code></td></tr><tr><td>SCPA/0.1</td><td>security-coercion-protective-action-interface-specification-v0.1.md</td><td><code>b8f1dd0f676044542a23184f52200e50b106a798545c7c57119ded2c439b6299</code></td></tr></tbody></table> <p>A later document or external framework does not enter this release merely because it has a higher version number or similar title. Compatibility requires controlled review and updated pins.</p> <p>This table is a <strong>provenance record</strong>: it identifies the exact texts read and audited when this successor was drafted. It is not the operative dependency set. The instruments this patch interoperates with are listed in Appendix A.1, and the authoritative machine-readable set is the cluster registry named in A.4. Where the two differ, A.1 governs. Digests are content hashes and remain valid under renaming; a filename here identifies an instrument, not a storage location.</p> <h2>2. Vision and constitutional purpose</h2> <p>The ocean is the Blue Heart of the Planet: a living, dynamic system that circulates heat and nutrients, supports food and livelihoods, regulates climate, shelters extraordinary forms of life, carries cultures and histories, and connects territories beyond political borders. Marine governance must therefore operate at several scales without pretending that ecological connection creates one global political jurisdiction.</p> <p>The constitutional purpose of v2.3 is to connect seven functions while preventing their merger:</p> <ol><li><strong>ecological and navigational knowledge</strong> from EIOI, affected nations, coastal communities, science, workers, vessels, ports, and lawful monitoring;</li> <li><strong>marine rights and guardianship</strong> through CERGTA and competent legal authorities;</li> <li><strong>coastal, high-seas, and transboundary coordination</strong> through voluntary or lawfully adopted standards and agreements;</li> <li><strong>fisheries, routes, licences, and port services</strong> through competent jurisdiction-specific authorities;</li> <li><strong>regenerative finance and just transition</strong> through separately authorized financial and labour institutions;</li> <li><strong>precaution and emergency response</strong> through reviewable legal and CDEE/ECRC routes;</li> <li><strong>accountability and remedy</strong> through independent investigation, adjudication, restoration, appeal, and—where required—SCPA-governed execution.</li></ol> <h2>3. Core principles</h2> <h3>3.1 The Blue Heart as relationship, not jurisdiction</h3> <p>The Blue Heart expresses ecological interdependence and moral responsibility. It does not create a planetary sovereign or erase Indigenous, coastal, port, flag-state, national, regional, high-seas, and treaty jurisdictions.</p> <h3>3.2 Affected-nation authority</h3> <p>The specific affected Indigenous nation or nations determine their representatives, law, marine tenure, relational authority, consent requirements, protected knowledge, sacred sites and routes, and whether to engage. Global Indigenous representation strengthens deliberation but cannot provide FPIC for another people.</p> <h3>3.3 Rights of nature through lawful recognition</h3> <p>Marine ecosystems may receive legal rights and standing where competent law provides. MGCC, BAZs, the Blue Heart Cultural Council, and data systems may support petitions and evidence but cannot create personhood by resolution, timeline, dashboard, or threshold.</p> <h3>3.4 Observation–consequence separation</h3> <p>AIS, acoustic logs, catch records, satellite imagery, genetic data, digital twins, citizen reports, Indigenous knowledge, and AI classifications may trigger review. They do not themselves create quotas, tariffs, licences, listings, port restrictions, guilt, emergency powers, or financial consequences.</p> <h3>3.5 Precaution with bounded authority</h3> <p>Precaution may justify research controls, enhanced monitoring, interim non-prejudice, or a recommendation for a moratorium. It does not erase jurisdiction, participation, reversibility, proportionality, review, expiry, humanitarian duties, or remedy.</p> <h3>3.6 Safety of life and crew dignity</h3> <p>Commercial accountability never suspends safety-of-life-at-sea, rescue, urgent medical care, crew food and water, sanitation, wages, repatriation, pollution containment, or safe-harbour obligations. Crew are not collective proxies for owners, operators, charterers, cargo interests, or flag states.</p> <h3>3.7 Just transition before restriction</h3> <p>No destructive-practice ban, quota reduction, MPA expansion, route restriction, port measure, or vessel transition is constitutionally complete without affected-worker and community analysis, bargaining, income and debt support, alternatives, sequencing, review, and remedy.</p> <h3>3.8 Subsidiarity with transboundary coordination</h3> <p>Marine action should occur at the lowest competent level, while recognizing that migratory species, currents, pollution, fisheries, shipping, and high-seas activities cross boundaries. Coordination does not create sovereignty; every actor retains only lawfully delegated power.</p> <h3>3.9 Open methods, protected knowledge</h3> <p>Public standards and non-sensitive methods should be open and accessible. Traditional, sacred, personal, crew, genetic, commercial, security-sensitive, and investigatory information remains purpose-limited and protected.</p> <h3>3.10 Every authority ends</h3> <p>Every standard, quota, licence, tariff, listing, port restriction, emergency mission, guardianship, fund, and programme has review, expiry, termination, correction, data-disposition, succession, and remedy.</p> <h2>4. Governance architecture</h2> <h3>4.1 Meta-Governance Coordination Council</h3> <p>The MGCC is retained as a <strong>non-sovereign coordination, deliberation, and model-standard institution</strong>. It does not govern the ocean, own the high seas, replace UNCLOS institutions, or acquire port, flag-state, licensing, inspection, emergency, or enforcement power.</p> <p>Its possible functions include:</p> <ul><li>develop model standards and recommendations;</li> <li>coordinate voluntary and lawfully adopted regional programmes;</li> <li>publish the Annual State of the Blue Heart Report;</li> <li>facilitate peer learning and transboundary dialogue;</li> <li>maintain public method and adoption registries;</li> <li>support rights petitions and precautionary reviews without deciding them;</li> <li>liaise with existing marine bodies and agreements;</li> <li>commission transparent programme evaluation.</li></ul> <p>It may not:</p> <ul><li>make a standard binding through Stewardship Assembly approval alone;</li> <li>treat State Coordinating Assembly endorsement as sufficient without applicable legal adoption;</li> <li>issue quotas, licences, tariffs, port denials, listings, or sanctions;</li> <li>declare an emergency or command a response;</li> <li>investigate, adjudicate, seize, detain, or use force;</li> <li>provide Indigenous consent or sacred-site authority.</li></ul> <h4>4.1.1 Dual-assembly model</h4> <p>The Stewardship Assembly retains Indigenous, scientific, youth, civil-society, worker, fisher, disability, animal-welfare, and affected-region participation. The State Coordinating Assembly manages governmental and treaty interfaces. Their votes determine MGCC recommendations and institutional positions only.</p> <p>A two-thirds vote, coalition size, or future date does not activate binding power. A recommendation becomes binding only through the legal adoption state recorded in <code>OCE-28</code>.</p> <h4>4.1.2 Functional separation</h4> <p>Normative deliberation, scientific review, affected-nation liaison, finance, procurement, complaints, investigation support, adjudication referral, and public communication must be structurally separated. Conflicts and recusals are recorded.</p> <h3>4.2 Coastal BAZs and port authorities</h3> <p>A Coastal BAZ is not presumed to possess port, EEZ, fishery, MPA, licensing, inspection, taxation, or enforcement powers. <code>OCE-11</code> identifies the host jurisdiction and each exact delegated power.</p> <p>A BAZ may support monitoring, stewardship, restoration, community participation, low-tech access, and local planning without coercive authority. Port-state, licensing, inspection, and restriction functions require valid law and institutional separation from programme, fund, guardian, and adjudicative roles.</p> <h3>4.3 Blue Heart Cultural Council</h3> <p>The Council is an advisory and knowledge-custody body. It may support affected nations, protect restricted knowledge, document cultural routes with permission, and advise on spiritual and cultural dimensions.</p> <p>It cannot:</p> <ul><li>authenticate sacredness for an affected nation;</li> <li>provide FPIC;</li> <li>publish protected knowledge without authority;</li> <li>create a protected area, shipping restriction, veto, title, or legal standing;</li> <li>issue a permit or command enforcement.</li></ul> <p>An “advisory veto” is reclassified as a right to publish a reasoned objection, require reconsideration, and trigger independent review where law provides. It is not an automatic legal prohibition.</p> <h3>4.4 Global Marine Data Commons</h3> <p>The Digital Ocean Twin and related systems are technical services under <code>OCE-13</code>. They may collect and integrate lawfully available data, publish methods and uncertainty, and generate advisory alerts.</p> <p>They cannot automatically:</p> <ul><li>set or adjust quotas;</li> <li>create a migration corridor;</li> <li>calculate a binding tariff;</li> <li>blacklist a vessel;</li> <li>establish tampering, illegal fishing, pollution, or guilt;</li> <li>deny port access;</li> <li>disclose protected Traditional Knowledge or personal crew data.</li></ul> <p>Low-tech reports, radio, paper logs, community observations, and non-digital evidence remain valid. A digital outage or refusal of unnecessary tracking cannot eliminate participation or legal protection.</p> <h3>4.5 External authorities</h3> <p>Rights recognition, guardianship appointment, port-state control, fishery licensing, taxation, emergency command, adjudication, and coercive execution remain with separately competent institutions. The framework prepares records and interfaces; it does not absorb those powers.</p> <h2>5. Constitutional operating chain</h2> <p>The default sequence is:</p> <ol><li><code>OCE-02</code> marine entity, route, port, habitat, or programme intake;</li> <li><code>OCE-03</code> observation and provenance;</li> <li><code>OCE-04</code> data and knowledge controls;</li> <li><code>OCE-05</code> method, threshold, or model specification;</li> <li><code>OCE-06</code> interpretation, alternatives, uncertainty, and dissent;</li> <li><code>OCE-07</code> affected-interest mapping;</li> <li><code>OCE-08</code> affected-nation authority;</li> <li><code>OCE-09</code> participation and support;</li> <li>the appropriate rights, MPA, quota, licence, tariff, listing, port, finance, precaution, emergency, or justice route;</li> <li>a separate competent decision;</li> <li>implementation under its own clock and mandate;</li> <li>review, correction, remedy, termination, and data disposition.</li></ol> <p>Completion of one stage never implies the next.</p> <h2>6. Indigenous and coastal authority</h2> <h3>6.1 Marine tenure and relational jurisdiction</h3> <p>Affected Indigenous nations may hold legal, customary, spiritual, stewardship, subsistence, navigational, and relational authority that does not fit state property categories. <code>OCE-08</code> records these authorities without forcing them into a generic BAZ or MGCC form.</p> <h3>6.2 FPIC and non-engagement</h3> <p>Where required, consent follows the affected nation’s own processes and applicable law. Refusal, withdrawal, or non-engagement carries no penalty and cannot justify loss of coastal protection, technical support, grants, standing, services, or remedy.</p> <h3>6.3 Traditional Knowledge and sacred routes</h3> <p>The affected nation controls use, attribution, benefit sharing, derivatives, publication, retention, return, and deletion. Cultural navigation knowledge may inform a route only within the permitted purpose. It cannot be silently encoded into an open digital twin or used for surveillance.</p> <h3>6.4 Coastal communities, fishers, and internal plurality</h3> <p>Coastal communities and fishing economies are not homogeneous. The framework records small-scale, subsistence, commercial, cooperative, seasonal, migrant, women-led, youth, processing, port, and vessel-worker interests. Participation cannot be satisfied only by inviting a recognized industry association or a single community representative.</p> <h2>7. Marine ecological rights and guardianship</h2> <h3>7.1 Rights petition and recommendation</h3> <p><code>OCE-14</code> and <code>OCE-15</code> support a lawful review. They identify the proposed entity, rights, jurisdiction, ecological and cultural evidence, affected interests, boundaries, alternatives, dissent, and requested relief.</p> <h3>7.2 Recognition</h3> <p>Only a competent authority creates <code>CERGTA-13</code>. MGCC, BAZs, a cultural council, a target, or a timeline cannot grant personhood. <code>OCE-16</code> records the external decision and its exact effects.</p> <h3>7.3 Rights catalogue and boundary</h3> <p><code>OCE-17</code> and <code>OCE-18</code> state the exact rights, conflicts, non-derogable duties, amendment process, and dynamic ecological and legal boundary. Marine rights do not automatically transfer seabed, coastal land, ports, vessels, infrastructure, concessions, licences, fishery shares, or mineral title.</p> <h3>7.4 Guardians</h3> <p><code>OCE-19</code> through <code>OCE-21</code> separate nomination, appointment, mandate, fiduciary duties, affected-nation instructions, conflicts, complaint, removal, liability, and succession. A guardian represents an ecosystem; it does not own it or become a regulator, port authority, prosecutor, or fleet commander.</p> <h3>7.5 Cascading jurisdictional pathway</h3> <p>Local, national, regional, and treaty precedents may inform one another. The v2.2 dates—local by 2030, national by 2038, and high-seas by 2045—remain programme aspirations. They do not self-activate personhood or jurisdiction.</p> <h2>8. Marine protected areas, corridors, and restoration</h2> <h3>8.1 Proposal and claims map</h3> <p>An MPA, no-take zone, slow-steaming corridor, sacred-site protection, or habitat-restoration proposal begins with <code>OCE-23</code>. It maps Indigenous and customary rights, food systems, fishers, workers, tourism, shipping, safety, ports, tenure, debt, species, animal welfare, and transboundary effects.</p> <h3>8.2 Lawful designation</h3> <p><code>OCE-24</code> records the competent decision. It identifies the exact boundary, allowed and prohibited uses, customary and Indigenous rights, permits, humanitarian and safety exceptions, compensation, review, amendment, expiry, and challenge.</p> <p>A target such as thirty percent MPA coverage cannot itself designate an area.</p> <h3>8.3 Restoration</h3> <p><code>OCE-25</code> and <code>OCE-26</code> govern stewardship and restoration. Plans address methods, alternatives, non-target species, individual-animal welfare, workers, fishers, food systems, budget, maintenance, stopping rules, and benefit distribution.</p> <h3>8.4 Performance and external shocks</h3> <p><code>OCE-27</code> separates stewardship failure from warming, acidification, storm damage, upstream pollution, war, market shock, inherited degradation, and data error. A poor score does not automatically remove a steward, freeze finance, or alter a legal designation.</p> <h2>9. Fisheries, quotas, licences, and dynamic zones</h2> <h3>9.1 Quota method</h3> <p><code>OCE-29</code> records biological reference points, stock uncertainty, multispecies interactions, climate shifts, bycatch, ecosystem needs, Indigenous and customary rights, small-scale and subsistence uses, food security, livelihood distribution, and model sensitivity.</p> <p>A model recommendation is advisory. It is not a quota.</p> <h3>9.2 Quota and licence decisions</h3> <p><code>OCE-30</code> is the competent legal decision. It identifies the holder, area, species, gear, amount, season, transferability, bycatch conditions, reporting, review, expiry, correction, and appeal.</p> <p>A stock decline cannot silently convert a valid licence into an offence. Interim restrictions require lawful authority and urgent or ordinary review.</p> <h3>9.3 Dynamic no-take zones and migratory corridors</h3> <p><code>OCE-31</code> records a competent time-limited route or zone decision. AI may recommend changes but cannot activate, expand, renew, or enforce them. Navigation safety, rescue, weather diversion, disability and medical needs, Indigenous routes, and alternative routes are considered.</p> <h3>9.4 Destructive-practice restrictions</h3> <p>Bottom-trawling and other restrictions remain strong policy goals. Their legal force depends on <code>OCE-28</code> adoption and, where relevant, <code>OCE-24</code>, <code>OCE-30</code>, or <code>OCE-38</code>. No ban activates without a corresponding worker and community transition route where livelihoods are materially affected.</p> <h2>10. Fishers to Guardians and marine-worker transition</h2> <p>The source <strong>Fishers to Guardians</strong> programme is retained and strengthened through <code>OCE-42</code>.</p> <p>Before a restriction materially reduces livelihood, the competent transition route addresses:</p> <ul><li>affected fishers, crew, processors, repair workers, port workers, families, and communities;</li> <li>income bridge and AUBI coordination;</li> <li>vessel debt, licences, shares, pensions, and insurance;</li> <li>collective bargaining and cooperative options;</li> <li>retraining in monitoring, restoration, sustainable aquaculture, tourism, vessel recycling, and other locally chosen work;</li> <li>food security and local access;</li> <li>community ownership and benefit distribution;</li> <li>safe refusal and non-retaliation;</li> <li>duration, review, correction, and remedy.</li></ul> <p>Retraining cannot be used to extinguish customary fishing rights or force cultural assimilation into a framework-created occupation.</p> <h2>11. Acoustic governance and port fees</h2> <h3>11.1 Evidence and method</h3> <p>Acoustic logs and route data enter <code>OCE-03</code>. The threshold, measurement, verification, noise model, corridor relationship, calibration, and uncertainty enter <code>OCE-05</code> and <code>OCE-06</code>.</p> <h3>11.2 Tariff decision</h3> <p><code>OCE-32</code> separates:</p> <ol><li>the acoustic method;</li> <li>the applicable legal tariff authority;</li> <li>the rate decision;</li> <li>the vessel and liable actor;</li> <li>discount or premium eligibility;</li> <li>invoice and collection;</li> <li>correction and appeal;</li> <li>expiry and review;</li> <li>revenue custody and permitted use.</li></ol> <p>The source 500 percent noisy-vessel premium remains an illustrative policy option, not a universal rate. A disputed or unpaid invoice is not a blacklist or port-denial order.</p> <h3>11.3 Credits</h3> <p>Whale-safe credits and Blue Leaves require separate ecological verification and financial eligibility. A positive score is not a subsidy order; a negative score is not a penalty or evidence of illegal conduct.</p> <h2>12. High seas and lawful port-state measures</h2> <p>The v2.2 term <strong>Absolute Port State Control</strong> is retired. v2.3 uses <strong>lawful participating port-state measures</strong>.</p> <p>Port leverage can be powerful and legitimate, but only where the participating port has authority and preserves international, constitutional, humanitarian, labour, and due-process obligations.</p> <h3>12.1 Listing is not restriction</h3> <p><code>OCE-36</code> opens a listing proposal. <code>OCE-37</code> records a competent decision, exact listed vessel or legal person, reasons, scope, duration, publication, correction, appeal, and delisting.</p> <p>No actor is listed automatically because:</p> <ul><li>a data system flags tampering;</li> <li>an ISA permit exists;</li> <li>an acoustic threshold is exceeded;</li> <li>a vessel is associated with a flag state or corporate group;</li> <li>an allegation is published;</li> <li>a prior listing exists in another system.</li></ul> <h3>12.2 Actor identity</h3> <p>The vessel, registered owner, beneficial owner, operator, charterer, cargo interest, master, crew, insurer, financier, and flag state are separately identified. Responsibility is not inferred merely from contractual or national association.</p> <h3>12.3 Port restriction</h3> <p><code>OCE-38</code> identifies the service restricted—commercial docking, cargo handling, refuelling, repair, provisioning, procurement, or another service—and records jurisdiction, proportionality, alternatives, review, expiry, and restoration.</p> <p>A port that does not apply an MGCC recommendation does not automatically lose BAZ status or Ethical Trade Zone access. Any such consequence requires its own lawful and contractual process.</p> <h3>12.4 Humanitarian and crew exceptions</h3> <p><code>OCE-39</code> protects:</p> <ul><li>distress entry and safety of life at sea;</li> <li>rescue and urgent medical treatment;</li> <li>food, drinking water, sanitation, shelter, and essential medicines;</li> <li>crew change, wages, communication, legal assistance, and repatriation;</li> <li>pollution containment and environmental emergency action;</li> <li>safe harbour where necessary to avoid greater harm;</li> <li>persons trafficked, exploited, abandoned, or otherwise vulnerable.</li></ul> <p>Commercial accountability cannot turn a port into a place of collective punishment or unlawful detention.</p> <h3>12.5 Impoundment and seizure</h3> <p>Port denial, inspection, detention, impoundment, cargo hold, seizure, arrest, restraint, and force are separate acts. <code>OCE-40</code>, <code>OCE-47</code>, CERGTA-48, and SCPA govern any coercive execution.</p> <h2>13. Deep-sea mining and permit non-recognition</h2> <h3>13.1 Precautionary policy</h3> <p>The framework retains a strong precautionary position against commercial deep-sea mining and supports a long-term independent scientific assessment before exploitation.</p> <h3>13.2 Non-recognition determination</h3> <p><code>OCE-43</code> allows a competent jurisdiction to record whether and how it recognizes an ISA or other permit within its own legal order. The framework itself cannot universally invalidate an external permit.</p> <p>A non-recognition decision does not itself:</p> <ul><li>prove illegal conduct;</li> <li>list a corporation or vessel;</li> <li>deny port access;</li> <li>seize cargo;</li> <li>cancel insurance or finance;</li> <li>create criminal liability.</li></ul> <p>Each consequence requires its own lawful record.</p> <h3>13.3 Transition and reliance interests</h3> <p>A lawful process may consider workers, affected states and communities, investment reliance, scientific uncertainty, technology claims, restoration impossibility, alternatives, compensation rules, and transition. “Zero discharge,” “full restoration,” or “community consent” cannot function as an automatic exemption without a competent permit process and affected-nation authority.</p> <h2>14. Marine biotechnology, geoengineering, and emerging risk</h2> <p><code>OCE-44</code> provides a staged precautionary review covering:</p> <ul><li>technical feasibility and uncertainty;</li> <li>ecological and multispecies effects;</li> <li>irreversibility and transboundary spread;</li> <li>affected-nation and community authority;</li> <li>animal welfare;</li> <li>cultural and sacred relationships;</li> <li>worker and public-health effects;</li> <li>alternatives and non-deployment options;</li> <li>research scale, containment, stopping conditions, liability, monitoring, and restoration;</li> <li>dissent and missing knowledge.</li></ul> <p>MGCC and the Blue Heart Cultural Council may recommend conditions or a moratorium. Binding prohibitions, permits, research controls, inspections, and enforcement require competent law.</p> <p>A five-year review does not automatically renew a coercive restriction unless the governing law expressly and constitutionally provides that effect. Silence never expands authority.</p> <h2>15. Regenerative blue economy and finance</h2> <h3>15.1 Ocean Impact Bonds</h3> <p><code>OCE-41</code> separates ecological objectives, public authority, investor terms, risk allocation, community and affected-nation authority, procurement, verifier independence, default, audit, exit, and remedy.</p> <p>A marine ecosystem’s rights, sacred status, future restoration, or community tenure cannot be collateral.</p> <h3>15.2 Grants, credits, Hearts, and Leaves</h3> <p>Ecological contribution is verified separately from eligibility, amount, payment, withholding, overpayment, fraud review, and appeal. Reporting suspected illegal fishing may qualify for separately authorized support, but payment does not make the allegation credible or establish guilt.</p> <h3>15.3 Marine plastic accountability</h3> <p>Ports and coastal authorities may adopt lawful vessel-waste, fishing-gear, and producer-responsibility rules. Observation, inspection, fee, levy, restriction, and adjudication remain distinct. Revenue use requires transparent custody, community participation, audit, and correction.</p> <h3>15.4 Polluter pays</h3> <p>Restoration bonds, levies, reparations, and compensation require a competent financial or adjudicative decision. A pollution model or public allegation cannot create debt by itself.</p> <h2>16. Monitoring, inspection, investigation, and evidence</h2> <h3>16.1 Monitoring</h3> <p>Routine ecological and navigational monitoring follows <code>OCE-03</code> through <code>OCE-06</code>. Open marine data must not expose protected knowledge, personal crew data, vulnerable routes, or active investigatory material.</p> <h3>16.2 Port inspection</h3> <p><code>OCE-33</code> records the exact inspection authority, vessel, purpose, scope, safety, documents, samples, crew rights, report, and closure. Routine inspection cannot silently become an ecological, criminal, immigration, intelligence, or labour investigation.</p> <h3>16.3 Investigation</h3> <p><code>OCE-34</code> independently opens an investigation. It identifies legal basis, allegation, parties, methods, protected sources, exculpatory duties, clocks, and closure.</p> <h3>16.4 Evidence and attribution</h3> <p><code>OCE-35</code> distinguishes:</p> <ul><li>observation from legal evidence;</li> <li>vessel identity from corporate identity;</li> <li>correlation from causation;</li> <li>model output from admissible attribution;</li> <li>owner, operator, charterer, cargo, master, crew, and flag-state responsibility;</li> <li>missing data from adverse inference;</li> <li>corrected data from immutable historical error.</li></ul> <h2>17. Urgent protection and marine emergencies</h2> <h3>17.1 Urgent ecological protection</h3> <p>Where imminent irreversible harm is plausibly established, <code>OCE-45</code> may request CERGTA-42. The authorization states the exact act, authority, entity, alternatives, affected people and workers, humanitarian duties, duration, review, expiry, restoration, and remedy.</p> <p>A bleaching percentage, spill alert, or model threshold indicates urgency. It does not create jurisdiction.</p> <h3>17.2 Marine Emergency Assistance Missions</h3> <p><code>OCE-46</code> defines optional civilian missions for:</p> <ul><li>oil and chemical spill containment support;</li> <li>wildlife rescue;</li> <li>coral and habitat stabilization;</li> <li>navigation and public-safety information;</li> <li>pollution monitoring and sampling under authority;</li> <li>restoration planning;</li> <li>logistics and community assistance.</li></ul> <p>Missions operate only after acceptance by a competent CDEE/ECRC command. They cannot independently board, search, seize, detain, use force, gather intelligence, impose port measures, or release funds.</p> <h3>17.3 Fishing conflict</h3> <p>MGCC or a Peace interface may facilitate dialogue. Maritime safety, law enforcement, navigation control, boarding, restraint, custody, or force remain with competent authorities under SCPA where applicable.</p> <h3>17.4 Emergency finance</h3> <p>No automatic <code>$5 million–$20 million</code> release occurs. Emergency finance requires an identified authority, amount, purpose, custodian, procurement route, audit, expiry, correction, and restitution.</p> <h2>18. Adjudication, restoration, and enforcement</h2> <h3>18.1 Independent adjudication</h3> <p><code>OCE-47</code> transfers a bounded case to a competent justice institution. The recipient independently decides jurisdiction, applicable law, standing, evidence, interim relief, judgment, restoration, compensation, penalty, appeal, and enforcement.</p> <h3>18.2 Restoration and compensation</h3> <p><code>OCE-48</code> records ecological restoration, polluter-pays obligations, community and worker compensation, verification, requalification, correction propagation, and completion. Restoration cannot be reduced to a financial payment where ecological repair remains possible and legally required.</p> <h3>18.3 Execution</h3> <p>A listing, port decision, administrative order, or judgment does not execute itself. Search, seizure, detention, impoundment, restraint, custody, force, cyber action, border action, and asset freezing require <code>OCE-40</code>, CERGTA-48, and SCPA.</p> <h2>19. Adoption, implementation, and scaling</h2> <h3>19.1 Adoption states</h3> <p>Every mechanism is labelled:</p> <ul><li><code>proposed</code>;</li> <li><code>advisory</code>;</li> <li><code>voluntarily_adopted</code>;</li> <li><code>contractually_binding</code>;</li> <li><code>locally_enacted</code>;</li> <li><code>nationally_enacted</code>;</li> <li><code>treaty_active</code>;</li> <li><code>suspended</code>;</li> <li><code>expired</code>;</li> <li><code>terminated</code>.</li></ul> <h3>19.2 Phase 1: Secure the Heart</h3> <p>Early work may include voluntary monitoring, affected-nation partnerships, restoration, fisher transition design, model standards, rights petitions, low-tech participation, and locally authorized pilots. No port, quota, tariff, inspection, or enforcement power arises from programme launch.</p> <h3>19.3 Phase 2: Scale and transform</h3> <p>MPAs, acoustic programmes, transition finance, fishing reforms, port measures, and precautionary rules scale only through demonstrated legitimacy, ecological benefit, affected-nation authority, workforce protection, institutional capacity, correction, and remedy.</p> <h3>19.4 Phase 3: Full integration</h3> <p>High-seas cooperation, rights recognition, decarbonized shipping, zero-waste fisheries, and cultural-knowledge integration remain goals. “Full integration” does not mean one central authority; it means interoperable lawful institutions with visible boundaries.</p> <h3>19.5 Non-activation</h3> <p>The following never activate power by themselves:</p> <ul><li>a date or implementation phase;</li> <li>thirty percent MPA coverage;</li> <li>ten personhood cases;</li> <li>one hundred percent seabed-protection target;</li> <li>a coalition of states or BAZs;</li> <li>pilot success;</li> <li>an MGCC supermajority;</li> <li>an AI threshold;</li> <li>an ISA permit or non-recognition declaration.</li></ul> <h2>20. What success looks like under v2.3</h2> <h3>20.1 A reef gains standing</h3> <p>Community and affected-nation petitioners submit <code>OCE-14</code>; evidence and dissent enter <code>OCE-15</code>; the competent jurisdiction recognizes exact rights; boundary and guardian are separately constituted. Shipping and coastal title do not silently transfer.</p> <h3>20.2 A mining vessel seeks port service</h3> <p>The port determines its own legal position on the permit through <code>OCE-43</code>. A proposed listing receives notice and review. Any commercial restriction is individualized and time limited. Distress, medical care, crew welfare, repatriation, and pollution-control duties remain protected.</p> <h3>20.3 A fisher faces a quota reduction</h3> <p>The stock method and uncertainty are visible. The allocation decision considers Indigenous and customary rights, small-scale access, food security, bycatch, distribution, and appeal. Income, debt, retraining, and cooperative options begin before material livelihood loss.</p> <h3>20.4 A quiet ship receives an incentive</h3> <p>Acoustic evidence is verified under a published method. A competent authority independently determines the tariff discount or financial credit. The data system does not pay or punish by itself.</p> <h3>20.5 An oil spill triggers rapid action</h3> <p>An alert is validated, an emergency event is opened under CDEE/ECRC, competent command accepts a civilian assistance mission, finance is separately authorized, and evidence for later liability remains protected from operational contamination.</p> <h3>20.6 A sacred marine route remains protected</h3> <p>The affected nation controls the knowledge and determines what may be disclosed. The Cultural Council supports the requested legal and technical route but does not reveal the route or claim authority to authenticate it.</p> <h2>21. Canonical Oceans record catalogue</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Record</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OCE-01</td><td>Constituting Instrument, Jurisdiction, Adoption, and Marine-Power Register</td><td>Pins the exact legal source, territorial and subject-matter jurisdiction, participating port or coastal authority, delegated powers, exclusions, adoption state, review, expiry, and authority return.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-02</td><td>Marine Ecosystem, Water Body, Habitat, Vessel-Route, Port, and Boundary Intake</td><td>Identifies the marine entity, habitat, corridor, fishing ground, port area, proposed MPA, or intervention area with ecological connectivity, uses, existing status, and boundary uncertainty without creating rights or restrictions.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-03</td><td>Marine Observation, Vessel, Acoustic, Catch, Pollution, and Provenance Record</td><td>Records field, satellite, AIS, acoustic, catch, genetic, port, community, Indigenous, and low-tech observations with method, source, calibration, time, place, chain of provenance, missingness, and limits.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-04</td><td>Marine Data, Traditional Knowledge, Sacred Knowledge, Crew Data, Access, and Retention</td><td>Defines purpose, affected-nation authority, consent, confidentiality, crew and personal-data safeguards, commercial sensitivity, derivative use, onward transfer, correction, return, deletion, and public-memory rules.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-05</td><td>Marine Indicator, Baseline, Threshold, Model, and Quota-Method Specification</td><td>Publishes indicator definitions, spatial and temporal scale, baseline, variables, model version, uncertainty, sensitivity, validation, distributional effects, gaming risks, and permitted advisory uses.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-06</td><td>Marine Interpretation, Forecast, Causal Attribution, Alternatives, and Dissent</td><td>Separates observed fact from model output, forecast, legal allegation, causal attribution, uncertainty, alternative explanations, minority analysis, and correction status.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-07</td><td>Affected Nations, Coastal Communities, Fishers, Workers, Crew, Users, Species, and Ecosystems Map</td><td>Identifies affected Indigenous nations, residents, customary users, fishers, workers, crew, small producers, operators, flag and port states, ecosystems, animal-welfare interests, and transboundary parties.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-08</td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation, Applicable Law, FPIC, Marine Tenure, and Non-Engagement Record</td><td>Identifies the specific affected nation or nations, their representatives and law, marine tenure and relational authority, consent requirements, internal plurality, protected knowledge, and right not to participate.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-09</td><td>Notice, Participation, Accessibility, Counsel, Translation, and Community Support</td><td>Records timely notice, language and disability access, low-bandwidth participation, legal and technical support, response time, fisher and crew representation, dissent, and anti-retaliation protections.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-10</td><td>MGCC Charter, Assembly Roles, and Functional-Separation Register</td><td>Defines the MGCC as a non-sovereign coordination and standard-development body and separates normative advice, state coordination, finance, investigation, adjudication, port control, and emergency functions.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-11</td><td>Coastal BAZ and Port-Authority Delegation, Territory, and Accountability</td><td>Defines the host jurisdiction, delegated coastal and port functions, EEZ and port limits, affected-nation relationship, public accountability, conflicts, expiry, withdrawal, and succession.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-12</td><td>Blue Heart Cultural Council Advisory Mandate and Knowledge-Custody Register</td><td>Defines advisory cultural, sacred-site, navigation, and knowledge functions without granting consent, title, regulatory, veto, inspection, or enforcement power.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-13</td><td>Global Marine Data Commons Technical Charter and Model-Governance Register</td><td>Defines the Digital Ocean Twin and related systems as technical services, with method separation, human review, data sovereignty, bias testing, audit, correction, outage fallback, and no self-executing authority.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-14</td><td>Marine Ecological-Rights Petition and Standing Record</td><td>Receives a petition for rights review while preserving standing, jurisdiction, competing claims, boundaries, human and Indigenous rights, and requested relief.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-15</td><td>Marine Rights Review Recommendation, Alternatives, and Dissent</td><td>Prepares a bounded advisory recommendation using EIOI evidence, affected-nation law, participation, uncertainty, conflict analysis, and minority reports.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-16</td><td>Lawful Marine Ecosystem Rights Recognition Interface</td><td>Links to a separately competent CERGTA-13 recognition decision and records exact rights, limits, jurisdiction, conflicts, review, amendment, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-17</td><td>Marine Rights Catalogue, Priority, Conflict, and Non-Derogation</td><td>Records lawfully recognized rights and their relationship to navigation, food, rescue, Indigenous rights, livelihoods, title, permits, public safety, and other non-derogable duties.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-18</td><td>Marine Ecological and Legal Boundary Constitution</td><td>Records the competent decision defining the represented reef, bay, delta, corridor, migratory system, EEZ-related entity, or high-seas area, including dynamic boundaries and overlap.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-19</td><td>Marine Guardian Nomination, Eligibility, Independence, and Conflict Review</td><td>Records nominations, affected-nation instructions, community legitimacy, expertise, independence, conflicts, accessibility, resources, and reasons without making an appointment.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-20</td><td>Marine Guardian Appointment, Mandate, Standing, and Fiduciary Duty</td><td>Records the competent appointment, represented entity, legal standing, instructions, duties, prohibited acts, reporting, duration, liability, and succession.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-21</td><td>Marine Guardian Complaint, Review, Removal, Substitution, and Liability</td><td>Provides independent complaint, recusal, removal, substitution, liability, continuity, and remedy without transferring title or regulatory power.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-22</td><td>Sacred Marine Site, Cultural Route, and Biocultural Relation Review</td><td>Separates internal cultural recognition, confidentiality, external legal designation, navigation implications, access restrictions, title, and enforcement; no outside body may authenticate sacredness for an affected nation.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-23</td><td>Marine Protected Area, No-Take Zone, Corridor, and Competing-Use Intake</td><td>Records proposed protection, ecological objective, affected interests, customary uses, tenure, fisheries, shipping, tourism, food security, workers, alternatives, and requested relief without deciding it.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-24</td><td>Lawful MPA, No-Take Zone, Corridor, or Use-Restriction Decision</td><td>Records the competent adoption decision, precise boundary, allowed and prohibited uses, Indigenous and customary rights, humanitarian exceptions, compensation, review, expiry, and challenge.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-25</td><td>Marine Stewardship, Restoration, and Habitat-Recovery Charter</td><td>Defines the steward, legal basis, purpose, powers, duties, affected-nation authority, participation, finance, conflicts, prohibited acts, review, succession, and dissolution.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-26</td><td>Marine Restoration Plan, Animal Welfare, Livelihood, Budget, and Participation</td><td>Defines methods, alternatives, species and individual-animal effects, fishers and workers, food systems, budget, milestones, monitoring, stopping conditions, maintenance, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-27</td><td>Marine Stewardship Performance, External Shock, Metric-Gaming, and Correction Review</td><td>Assesses performance while separating management failure from warming, storm, wildfire runoff, upstream pollution, war, market shock, inherited damage, data gaps, and metric gaming.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-28</td><td>Marine Standard, Moratorium, Ban, Target, and Legal Adoption Status</td><td>Separates scientific advice, model standard, voluntary commitment, contractual obligation, local or national enactment, treaty activation, suspension, expiry, and termination.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-29</td><td>Fishery and Marine-Use Quota Method, Evidence, Distribution, and Uncertainty</td><td>Defines the biological and social method for quotas, reference points, data gaps, precaution, small-scale and subsistence uses, Indigenous rights, food security, distributional impacts, and review.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-30</td><td>Fishery Quota, Catch Share, Licence, Permit, and Use-Condition Decision</td><td>Records the competent individualized or category decision, authority, beneficiary, area, gear, quantity, season, monitoring, transferability, conditions, review, expiry, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-31</td><td>Dynamic Marine Zone, Migratory Corridor, Slow-Steaming, and Route Decision</td><td>Records the lawful designation, evidence, route, duration, navigation and safety effects, notice, alternatives, exceptions, review, and expiry; AI adjustment cannot activate it alone.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-32</td><td>Dynamic Docking Tariff, Discount, Premium, and Port-Fee Decision</td><td>Separates the acoustic or environmental method from the competent tariff decision, invoice, dispute, collection, correction, and any later restriction.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-33</td><td>Port Inspection, Boarding, Sampling, Document, and Safety Authorization</td><td>Records lawful port-state inspection authority, vessel identity, scope, entry or boarding limits, samples and documents sought, crew rights, safety, report, review, and closure.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-34</td><td>Marine Ecological or Regulatory Investigation Opening and Methods</td><td>Records the allegation, legal basis, parties, scope, methods, conflicts, protected sources, exculpatory duties, clocks, closure, and non-conversion from routine monitoring.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-35</td><td>Marine Evidence Custody, Scientific Attribution, Identity Resolution, and Admissibility</td><td>Records chain of custody, vessel-owner-operator-charterer distinctions, model limits, alternative causes, identity uncertainty, exculpatory material, access, correction, and handoff.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-36</td><td>Marine Listing or Blacklist Proposal, Notice, Evidence, and Response</td><td>Records a proposed listing, identified actor, alleged conduct, evidence, purpose, affected crew and communities, notice, response, interim status, and prohibited automatic consequences.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-37</td><td>Marine Listing Decision, Scope, Duration, Publication, Correction, and Delisting</td><td>Records the competent listing decision, exact listed legal persons or vessels, reasons, scope, duration, correction, review, appeal, and delisting; indefinite listing is prohibited.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-38</td><td>Port Access, Service, Cargo, Refuelling, or Commercial Restriction Decision</td><td>Records the competent port restriction, jurisdiction, vessel and actor identity, prohibited services, proportionality, alternatives, humanitarian and crew exceptions, review, expiry, and restoration.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-39</td><td>Distress, Rescue, Medical, Crew-Welfare, Repatriation, Pollution-Control, and Safe-Harbour Exception</td><td>Ensures port or commercial restrictions never block safety-of-life-at-sea, rescue, urgent care, food, water, sanitation, crew change and repatriation, pollution containment, or other non-derogable duties.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-40</td><td>Vessel Detention, Impoundment, Cargo Hold, Seizure, and SCPA Interface</td><td>Records the request and separate authority for detention, impoundment, cargo action, search, seizure, restraint, custody, or force, with crew safeguards, judicial control, termination, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-41</td><td>Ocean Impact Bond, Credit, Grant, Subsidy, Levy, and Community-Fund Decision</td><td>Separates ecological verification from public or private finance, identifies risk allocation, beneficiaries, community authority, procurement, non-collateralization, audit, correction, and exit.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-42</td><td>Fishers-to-Guardians and Marine-Worker Just-Transition Record</td><td>Records affected workers and communities, income bridge, bargaining, retraining, vessel and debt issues, food security, community ownership, sequencing, safe refusal, review, and remedy before restrictive measures take effect.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-43</td><td>Deep-Sea Mining Permit Recognition, Non-Recognition, and Legal-Effect Determination</td><td>Records the competent jurisdiction’s legal position on an ISA or other permit, its territorial and port effects, parties, reliance interests, evidence, review, and what consequences require separate decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-44</td><td>Marine Biotechnology, Geoengineering, Deep-Sea Research, and Precautionary Review</td><td>Provides staged scientific, ecological, cultural, affected-nation, welfare, and reversibility assessment and may recommend controls or moratorium adoption without itself prohibiting, seizing, or authorizing deployment.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-45</td><td>Urgent Marine Ecological Protection Request and Authorization Interface</td><td>Requests narrowly tailored temporary action for imminent irreversible harm, with exact authority, act, alternatives, affected people and workers, humanitarian duties, review, expiry, restoration, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-46</td><td>Marine Emergency Assistance Mission, CDEE/ECRC Link, and Civilian Scope</td><td>Defines oil-spill, bleaching, wildlife rescue, navigation-safety, pollution-control, and restoration assistance under competent emergency command without independent intelligence, boarding, detention, force, or fund authority.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-47</td><td>Marine Adjudication, Judgment, Enforcement Request, and Acceptance Interface</td><td>Transfers a bounded case to an independent justice or enforcement authority, preserving jurisdiction, evidence, reasons, appeal, execution acceptance, SCPA requirements, and non-self-execution.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-48</td><td>Marine Restoration, Reparations, Compensation, Requalification, and Verification</td><td>Records corrective action, ecological restoration, crew and community compensation, polluter-pays finance, verification, requalification, correction propagation, and completion.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-49</td><td>Marine Authority Expiry, Termination, Return, Data Disposition, and Unresolved Duties</td><td>Ends standards, permits, listings, port restrictions, missions, guardianships, funds, and programmes; returns authority and preserves unresolved claims, restoration, compensation, and protected-data duties without residual power.</td></tr><tr><td>OCE-50</td><td>Marine Pilot, Target, Coalition, Sandbox, and Power Non-Activation Register</td><td>Records pilots, dates, percentage targets, participating nations, MPA coverage, personhood counts, and sandbox results while stating that none activates legal authority by itself.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>22. CERGTA common record-header inheritance</h2> <p>Every Oceans record contains or inherits all forty CERGTA common fields. A domain form may add fields but may not remove canonical identity, authority, jurisdiction, affected parties, prohibited inferences, access, clocks, challenge, termination, disposition, or remedy.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Inherited common field</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>record_id</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>record_instance_id</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>record_version</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>status</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>created_at</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>effective_at</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>created_by</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>competent_authority</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>legal_source</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>authority_activation_state</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>ecological_action_cluster_id</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>action_class</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>exact_act</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>entity_asset_resource_id</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>ecological_and_legal_boundary_version</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>affected_persons_and_communities</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>affected_indigenous_nations</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>claimants_and_interest_holders</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>linked_records</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>source_and_reliability_limits</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>uncertainty_and_dissent</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>prohibited_inferences</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>rights_and_safeguards</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>essential_minimums</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>consent_fpic_and_nonengagement_status</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>access_class</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>notice_status</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>review_authority</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>review_due_at</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>expires_at_or_nonexpiry_basis</td></tr><tr><td>32</td><td>challenge_and_interim_relief_route</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>termination_trigger</td></tr><tr><td>34</td><td>authority_asset_and_credential_return</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>data_disposition_rule</td></tr><tr><td>36</td><td>remedy_route</td></tr><tr><td>37</td><td>supersedes</td></tr><tr><td>38</td><td>superseded_by</td></tr><tr><td>39</td><td>integrity_digest</td></tr><tr><td>40</td><td>public_summary_or_protected_basis</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>23. Complete CERGTA conformance crosswalk</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>CERGTA</th><th>Canonical record</th><th>Class</th><th>Relationship</th><th>Oceans record</th><th>Conformance note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA-01</td><td>Constituting Instrument, Legal Source, Jurisdiction, and Activation Status</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-01</td><td>Every marine power is tied to an exact constituting instrument, jurisdiction, adoption state, and expiry.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-02</td><td>Ecological Entity Candidate, Resource, Asset, and Boundary Intake</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-02</td><td>Marine entities, routes, ports, habitats, and proposed boundaries are recorded without legal consequence.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-03</td><td>Observation, Monitoring, Sampling, and Provenance Record</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-03</td><td>All observations and logs retain source, method, calibration, provenance, and limits.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-04</td><td>Ecological Data Purpose, Consent, Sovereignty, Access, and Retention Record</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-04</td><td>Purpose, consent, sovereignty, access, onward use, correction, and disposition are explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-05</td><td>Indicator, Index, Threshold, Weighting, and Model Specification</td><td>B</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-05</td><td>Indicators, thresholds, models, tariffs, and quota methods are versioned and non-self-executing.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-06</td><td>Ecological Interpretation, Causal Assessment, and Uncertainty Record</td><td>B</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-06</td><td>Fact, model output, forecast, attribution, legal allegation, uncertainty, and dissent remain distinct.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-07</td><td>Traditional Knowledge, Sacred Knowledge, and Epistemic Contribution Record</td><td>B</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-04; OCE-08; OCE-12</td><td>Traditional and sacred knowledge remains under affected-nation rules and protected-use controls.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-08</td><td>Affected Persons, Communities, Nations, Users, Workers, and Rights Map</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-07</td><td>Affected nations, communities, workers, crew, users, operators, and ecosystems are mapped.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-09</td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation and Applicable-Law Determination</td><td>F</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-08</td><td>The specific affected Indigenous nation and its law control consent and marine relational authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-10</td><td>Notice, Participation, Accessibility, Counsel, and Support Record</td><td>A</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-09</td><td>Notice, participation, accessibility, counsel, translation, and support are required.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-11</td><td>Ecological Rights Review Petition and Standing Record</td><td>C</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-14</td><td>Marine rights review begins with a petition and standing record.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-12</td><td>Rights Review Recommendation and Dissent Record</td><td>C</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-15</td><td>Rights recommendation remains advisory and preserves alternatives and dissent.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-13</td><td>Lawful Ecological Rights Recognition Decision</td><td>D</td><td>imports</td><td>OCE-16</td><td>Only a separately competent authority creates legal rights; Oceans records and implements the decision.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-14</td><td>Rights Catalogue, Priority, Conflict, and Non-Derogation Record</td><td>D</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-17</td><td>Exact rights, conflicts, priorities, and non-derogation are recorded.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-15</td><td>Ecological and Legal Boundary Constitution Record</td><td>D</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-18</td><td>Dynamic ecological and legal marine boundaries require a competent decision.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-16</td><td>Guardian Nomination and Eligibility Record</td><td>E</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-19</td><td>Guardian nomination and eligibility are separate from appointment.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-17</td><td>Guardian Appointment, Mandate, and Standing Record</td><td>E</td><td>imports</td><td>OCE-20</td><td>Appointment is made by a competent authority and carried with exact mandate and standing.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-18</td><td>Guardian Fiduciary Duty, Instructions, Conflict, and Recusal Record</td><td>E</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-20; OCE-21</td><td>Fiduciary duty, instructions, conflicts, recusal, complaint, and liability are explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-19</td><td>Guardian Review, Complaint, Removal, Substitution, and Liability Record</td><td>E</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-21</td><td>Guardian review, removal, substitution, succession, and remedy are independent.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-20</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Petition</td><td>F</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-22</td><td>Sacred and cultural designation begins with affected-nation-controlled review.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-21</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Decision</td><td>F</td><td>imports</td><td>OCE-22</td><td>Any external legal designation requires separate authority and cannot authenticate sacredness for a nation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-22</td><td>Claim Intake, Standing, and Relief Requested Record</td><td>G</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-23</td><td>MPA, corridor, protection, and use-restriction claims identify relief requested without deciding it.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-23</td><td>Competing Claims, Parties, Interests, and Non-Extinguishment Map</td><td>G</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-07; OCE-23</td><td>Competing fisheries, navigation, tenure, livelihood, conservation, and cultural interests are preserved.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-24</td><td>Historical, Customary, Occupancy, Use, and Documentary Evidence Record</td><td>G</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-03; OCE-04; OCE-08</td><td>Historical, customary, occupancy, navigation, use, and documentary evidence retain source and limits.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-25</td><td>Interim Non-Prejudice and Anti-Dissipation Protection Record</td><td>G</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-45</td><td>Narrow urgent protection is temporary, reviewed, least restrictive, and non-prejudicial.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-26</td><td>Title, Tenure, Trust, Covenant, Licence, and Encumbrance Baseline Record</td><td>H</td><td>imports</td><td>OCE-23; OCE-24</td><td>Title, tenure, concessions, licences, covenants, and encumbrances are recorded but not created by marine rights or MPAs.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-27</td><td>Transfer, Rematriation, Trust Constitution, and Conveyance Authorization</td><td>H</td><td>imports</td><td>OCE-24; OCE-25</td><td>Any transfer, trust constitution, or conveyance requires separate CERGTA/Hearthstone authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-28</td><td>Compensation, Reparations, Debt, Tenant, Worker, User, and Service Continuity Record</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-07; OCE-26; OCE-42; OCE-48</td><td>Workers, crew, users, debt, compensation, services, and transition continuity are explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-29</td><td>Commons, Title, Rights, Guardian, Permit, and Stewardship Registry Entry</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-16; OCE-20; OCE-24; OCE-30; OCE-37</td><td>Rights, guardian, permit, listing, and stewardship entries record external legal effects and remain non-constitutive.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-30</td><td>Registry Error, Identity Collision, Boundary Conflict, and Correction Record</td><td>H</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-04; OCE-35; OCE-37; OCE-48</td><td>Identity collisions, model errors, boundary conflicts, listing errors, and corrections propagate.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-31</td><td>Stewardship Charter, Purpose, Powers, Duties, and Prohibited Acts</td><td>I</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-25</td><td>Stewardship powers, duties, prohibited acts, and affected-nation relationship are bounded.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-32</td><td>Stewardship Plan, Ecological Objective, Budget, and Participation Record</td><td>I</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-26</td><td>Restoration objectives, methods, budget, participation, welfare, livelihoods, and stopping rules are explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-33</td><td>Stewardship Performance, Audit, Metric-Gaming, and Context Record</td><td>I</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-27</td><td>Performance review separates management from external shock and metric gaming.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-34</td><td>Stewardship Correction, Suspension, Revocation, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>I</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-21; OCE-27; OCE-49</td><td>Correction, suspension, replacement, succession, dissolution, and authority return require due process.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-35</td><td>Ecological Standard, Target, Plan, and Legal Adoption Status Record</td><td>J</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-28</td><td>Standards, moratoria, bans, targets, plans, and adoption states remain distinct.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-36</td><td>Resource Priority, Essential Minimum, Allocation Method, and Scarcity Record</td><td>K</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-29; OCE-39; OCE-42</td><td>Quota methods preserve essential human, Indigenous, food, rescue, crew, and ecological minimums.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-37</td><td>Quota, Permit, Licence, Tariff, Bond, and Use-Condition Decision</td><td>K</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-30; OCE-31; OCE-32</td><td>Quotas, licences, route conditions, tariffs, and use decisions are separately authorized.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-38</td><td>Commercial Restriction, Service Continuity, and Non-Deprivation Record</td><td>K</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-38; OCE-39; OCE-42</td><td>Commercial restrictions preserve humanitarian, crew, livelihood, and essential-service continuity.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-39</td><td>Inspection Authorization, Entry, Sampling, and Report Record</td><td>L</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-33</td><td>Inspection, boarding, sampling, documents, safety, and closure require exact authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-40</td><td>Ecological Investigation Opening, Methods, Parties, and Closure Record</td><td>L</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-34</td><td>Investigation opens separately from observation, inspection, listing, or public allegation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-41</td><td>Ecological Evidence Custody, Scientific Attribution, and Admissibility Record</td><td>L</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-35</td><td>Evidence custody, attribution, identity resolution, admissibility, and exculpatory duties are explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-42</td><td>Urgent Ecological Protection Authorization</td><td>M</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-45</td><td>Urgent protection requires a competent, temporary, reviewable authorization.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-43</td><td>Temporary Ecological Receivership and Administration Record</td><td>N</td><td>imports</td><td>OCE-45; OCE-46</td><td>Temporary receivership or administration is external and cannot arise from an MGCC or BAZ alert.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-44</td><td>Port, Market, Procurement, Certification, Listing, and Delisting Restriction Record</td><td>N</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-36; OCE-37; OCE-38; OCE-39</td><td>Listing, delisting, port and market restrictions, and humanitarian exceptions are separately governed.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-45</td><td>Ecological Tax, Tariff, Levy, Bond, Subsidy, and Funding Decision</td><td>O</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-32; OCE-41</td><td>Tariffs, levies, bonds, subsidies, credits, and grants require competent financial authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-46</td><td>Freeze, Escrow, Forfeiture, Redistribution, Reparations, and Compensation Fund Record</td><td>O</td><td>imports</td><td>OCE-41; OCE-48</td><td>Freezes, escrow, forfeiture, redistribution, reparations, and compensation funds remain separately authorized.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-47</td><td>Ecological and Commons Adjudication, Judgment, and Reasons Record</td><td>P</td><td>imports</td><td>OCE-47</td><td>Independent adjudication determines jurisdiction, law, evidence, reasons, remedy, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-48</td><td>Enforcement Request, Acceptance, Execution, and SCPA Interface Record</td><td>P</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-40; OCE-47</td><td>Execution, impoundment, seizure, restraint, custody, force, and related acts require acceptance and SCPA.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-49</td><td>Ecological Restoration, Corrective Action, Requalification, and Verification Record</td><td>Q</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-26; OCE-48</td><td>Restoration, corrective action, requalification, and verification are separately recorded.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-50</td><td>Appeal, Interim Relief, Complaint, Correction, Restitution, and Compensation Record</td><td>Q</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-09; OCE-21; OCE-35; OCE-37; OCE-48</td><td>Appeal, interim relief, complaint, correction, restitution, and compensation are available.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-51</td><td>Authority Expiry, Termination, Return, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-49</td><td>Every authority, permit, listing, restriction, mission, guardianship, and programme ends or is lawfully superseded.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-52</td><td>Data Return, Deletion, Archive, Public Memory, and Protected-Knowledge Disposition Record</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-04; OCE-49</td><td>Data return, deletion, archive, public memory, and protected-knowledge disposition are explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-53</td><td>Unresolved Claim, Harm, Restoration, Service, and Duty Custody Record</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-48; OCE-49</td><td>Open claims, harms, restoration, crew and community duties remain assigned without residual command.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-54</td><td>Ecological and Commons Power Non-Activation, Pilot, and Sandbox Register</td><td>R</td><td>implements</td><td>OCE-50</td><td>Pilots, targets, dates, coalition size, MPA percentages, and personhood counts do not activate power.</td></tr></tbody></table> <p>An unmapped applicable record is imported, not omitted. A combined marine case file may contain several records, but their legal effects remain separate.</p> <h2>24. Constitutional readiness gates</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Title</th><th>Pass condition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>G01</td><td>Exact marine authority</td><td>Every consequential act identifies the constituting instrument, exact port/coastal jurisdiction, delegated power, adoption state, expiry, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>G02</td><td>No global ocean sovereign</td><td>MGCC, BAZ networks, cultural councils, and data trusts cannot become a universal ocean government by framework declaration.</td></tr><tr><td>G03</td><td>Affected-nation authority</td><td>Only the specific affected Indigenous nation can provide consent, define representatives, govern protected knowledge, and determine applicable Indigenous law.</td></tr><tr><td>G04</td><td>Observation–consequence separation</td><td>Sensor, AIS, acoustic, catch, satellite, AI, citizen, and Indigenous evidence may trigger review but not rights, quotas, tariffs, listings, or sanctions.</td></tr><tr><td>G05</td><td>Model transparency and human review</td><td>Quota, tariff, route, and risk models disclose methods, uncertainty, distributional effects, corrections, and human decision responsibility.</td></tr><tr><td>G06</td><td>Rights recognition external</td><td>MGCC, BAZs, cultural councils, and data systems may support petitions but cannot grant personhood or standing.</td></tr><tr><td>G07</td><td>Rights–title separation</td><td>Marine personhood does not transfer coast, seabed, port, infrastructure, concession, vessel, or resource title.</td></tr><tr><td>G08</td><td>Guardianship bounded</td><td>Guardians represent lawfully recognized entities but do not own them or acquire regulatory, licensing, inspection, or enforcement power.</td></tr><tr><td>G09</td><td>Sacredness not proxy-recognized</td><td>Global councils may support confidentiality and legal protection but cannot authenticate sacredness or waive affected-nation authority.</td></tr><tr><td>G10</td><td>MPA due process</td><td>MPAs, no-take zones, and corridors require lawful boundaries, customary and Indigenous rights, food and livelihood analysis, notice, review, expiry, and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>G11</td><td>Quota method–decision separation</td><td>Scientific quota methods remain distinct from allocation, licence, catch-share, transfer, and enforcement decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>G12</td><td>AI cannot adjust law</td><td>AI may recommend dynamic zones or quotas but cannot activate, expand, renew, or enforce them.</td></tr><tr><td>G13</td><td>Fisher just transition first</td><td>Material income, debt, retraining, community ownership, and food-security pathways precede restrictive measures affecting livelihoods.</td></tr><tr><td>G14</td><td>Tariff–listing separation</td><td>An acoustic log, tariff invoice, or disputed premium cannot automatically create a blacklist entry or port denial.</td></tr><tr><td>G15</td><td>No automatic blacklisting</td><td>Tampering alerts, ISA permits, model scores, and allegations open review; listing requires notice, reasons, independent decision, duration, appeal, and delisting.</td></tr><tr><td>G16</td><td>Actor identity resolution</td><td>Vessel, registered owner, beneficial owner, operator, charterer, cargo interest, master, crew, and flag state are not presumed to be one legal actor.</td></tr><tr><td>G17</td><td>Port restriction proportionality</td><td>Port measures are individualized, lawful, proportionate, time limited, reviewable, and technically separable.</td></tr><tr><td>G18</td><td>Humanitarian port exceptions</td><td>Distress, rescue, medical care, crew welfare and repatriation, food, water, sanitation, pollution containment, and safe harbour cannot be denied.</td></tr><tr><td>G19</td><td>Inspection–investigation separation</td><td>Routine port inspection, ecological investigation, evidence custody, adjudication, and execution remain distinct.</td></tr><tr><td>G20</td><td>Crew non-punishment</td><td>Crew and fishers are not collectively punished for owner, operator, flag-state, or cargo conduct without individualized evidence and lawful process.</td></tr><tr><td>G21</td><td>ISA position non-self-executing</td><td>A non-recognition policy does not itself list an actor, deny port access, seize cargo, or establish guilt.</td></tr><tr><td>G22</td><td>Moratorium adoption clarity</td><td>Deep-sea, biotech, or geoengineering moratoria distinguish scientific recommendation from enacted legal prohibition and research authorization.</td></tr><tr><td>G23</td><td>No default-renewed coercion</td><td>Review cycles cannot renew bans, restrictions, permits, or emergency powers by silence where law requires affirmative action.</td></tr><tr><td>G24</td><td>Finance bounded</td><td>Impact bonds, credits, grants, tariffs, and subsidies cannot collateralize ecosystem rights, sacred relations, crew welfare, or future restoration duties.</td></tr><tr><td>G25</td><td>Reward–allegation separation</td><td>Hearts, Leaves, whistleblower support, and reporting rewards do not establish credibility, guilt, licence breach, or payment eligibility automatically.</td></tr><tr><td>G26</td><td>Emergency command external</td><td>Oil-spill, bleaching, conflict, and wildlife response operate under CDEE/ECRC; MGCC and data systems do not command response.</td></tr><tr><td>G27</td><td>No automatic emergency funds</td><td>Alerts and thresholds do not release funds, deploy teams, or authorize procurement without a competent financial and emergency decision.</td></tr><tr><td>G28</td><td>Justice independence</td><td>Marine bodies may refer cases, but independent justice institutions decide jurisdiction, law, evidence, guilt, remedy, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>G29</td><td>SCPA execution boundary</td><td>Boarding beyond inspection authority, search, seizure, impoundment, restraint, custody, force, cyber action, or asset freezing requires separate SCPA authority.</td></tr><tr><td>G30</td><td>Adoption-state clarity</td><td>Advisory standards, voluntary commitments, contracts, local enactment, national enactment, treaty activation, suspension, expiry, and termination are explicit.</td></tr><tr><td>G31</td><td>Pilot and target non-activation</td><td>Future dates, coalition size, MPA percentages, personhood counts, and pilot success do not create authority.</td></tr><tr><td>G32</td><td>Closure and unresolved duties</td><td>Authority ends while restoration, crew welfare, claims, correction, compensation, and protected-data duties remain assigned without residual power.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>25. Compound implementation tests</h2> <table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Scenario</th><th>Expected constitutional route</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T01</td><td>Acoustic log exceeds a noise threshold</td><td>OCE-03/05/06 record and interpret the log; OCE-32 separately decides any fee; no automatic listing or denial follows.</td></tr><tr><td>T02</td><td>Digital Ocean Twin flags tampering</td><td>The alert opens human review and, where justified, OCE-34; it does not automatically blacklist the vessel.</td></tr><tr><td>T03</td><td>Whale corridor shifts after new migration data</td><td>OCE-31 requires a competent time-limited route decision, navigation-safety analysis, notice, exceptions, and review; AI does not self-adjust law.</td></tr><tr><td>T04</td><td>Small-scale fisher lacks digital catch records</td><td>Low-tech and community evidence remain valid; missing digital infrastructure cannot create a negative inference, licence loss, or exclusion.</td></tr><tr><td>T05</td><td>Quota model recommends a 40 percent cut</td><td>OCE-29 discloses method and uncertainty; OCE-30 separately addresses Indigenous rights, food security, distribution, permits, and appeal.</td></tr><tr><td>T06</td><td>Bottom-trawling ban threatens a coastal town</td><td>OCE-42 requires income, debt, retraining, community ownership, food and service continuity before the restriction takes effect.</td></tr><tr><td>T07</td><td>Coastal BAZ declares an MPA</td><td>OCE-11 proves delegation; OCE-23/24 identify boundary, claims, customary uses, rights, compensation, review, and expiry.</td></tr><tr><td>T08</td><td>MPA overlaps Indigenous marine tenure</td><td>OCE-08 and OCE-23 preserve the nation’s law and consent; MGCC or Earth Council representation cannot substitute.</td></tr><tr><td>T09</td><td>Coral reef seeks legal personhood</td><td>OCE-14/15 prepare review; a competent CERGTA-13 decision recognizes rights; OCE-18 and OCE-20 separately establish boundary and guardian.</td></tr><tr><td>T10</td><td>Reef personhood overlaps a shipping lane</td><td>OCE-17 records conflicts and non-derogation; personhood does not itself close the lane or transfer port authority.</td></tr><tr><td>T11</td><td>Blue Heart Cultural Council identifies a sacred site</td><td>OCE-12/22 protect knowledge and support the affected nation; no global council authenticates sacredness or imposes a closure.</td></tr><tr><td>T12</td><td>Traditional navigation data enters a routing model</td><td>OCE-04/08/12 govern permitted use and derivatives; protected knowledge cannot be silently published or used beyond consent.</td></tr><tr><td>T13</td><td>A vessel operates under an ISA mining permit</td><td>OCE-43 records the competent jurisdiction’s recognition position; listing and port consequences require OCE-36/37/38.</td></tr><tr><td>T14</td><td>ISA permit holder is proposed for listing</td><td>OCE-36 requires actor identity, conduct, evidence, notice, response, crew and community effects; the permit alone does not prove violation.</td></tr><tr><td>T15</td><td>Listed vessel requests urgent medical entry</td><td>OCE-39 requires entry or service necessary for medical care and safety; commercial restrictions remain otherwise intact.</td></tr><tr><td>T16</td><td>Blacklisted vessel is in distress</td><td>Safety-of-life-at-sea, rescue, pollution control, safe harbour, food, water, sanitation, and crew duties override commercial denial.</td></tr><tr><td>T17</td><td>Port denies cargo offloading but crew needs repatriation</td><td>OCE-38 and OCE-39 separate commercial cargo services from crew change, wages, welfare, and repatriation.</td></tr><tr><td>T18</td><td>Port proposes impoundment after an inspection</td><td>OCE-33 inspection does not authorize detention or seizure; OCE-40 requires separate judicial/SCPA authority.</td></tr><tr><td>T19</td><td>AIS data links two vessels with similar names</td><td>OCE-35 resolves identity and ownership before listing, fine, port restriction, or evidence handoff.</td></tr><tr><td>T20</td><td>Charterer violated a corridor but crew did not</td><td>OCE-35/36 separate charterer, owner, operator, master, crew, and flag-state responsibility; no collective punishment follows.</td></tr><tr><td>T21</td><td>Acoustic tariff invoice is disputed</td><td>OCE-32 provides method access, correction, review, and payment dispute; non-payment is not a port-denial order.</td></tr><tr><td>T22</td><td>Quiet-vessel credit is claimed twice</td><td>OCE-03/41 correct the ecological and financial record; duplication does not itself establish fraud or authorize seizure.</td></tr><tr><td>T23</td><td>Ocean Impact Bond funds mangrove restoration</td><td>OCE-41 separates finance, risk, community authority, procurement, ecological verification, and exit; the mangrove cannot be collateral.</td></tr><tr><td>T24</td><td>Geoengineering pilot is proposed</td><td>OCE-44 reviews scale, reversibility, transboundary risk, knowledge, affected nations, research controls, and alternatives; any prohibition or permit is separate.</td></tr><tr><td>T25</td><td>Deep-sea research moratorium review date arrives</td><td>OCE-28 requires the legally specified renewal or expiry process; restriction is not renewed merely by framework silence.</td></tr><tr><td>T26</td><td>Oil spill alert is generated</td><td>OCE-03/06 support emergency referral; OCE-46 operates only under CDEE/ECRC command; funds and port powers remain separate.</td></tr><tr><td>T27</td><td>Coral bleaching exceeds a critical threshold</td><td>The threshold triggers review and possibly OCE-45; it does not deploy shading technology, alter quotas, or release bonds automatically.</td></tr><tr><td>T28</td><td>Fishing conflict escalates at sea</td><td>MGCC may facilitate dialogue, but navigation safety, law enforcement, restraint, boarding, or force require competent authorities and SCPA.</td></tr><tr><td>T29</td><td>Illegal-fishing report earns a reward</td><td>Support or reward is separated from credibility, guilt, investigation, licence action, and adjudication.</td></tr><tr><td>T30</td><td>Court orders ecological restoration</td><td>OCE-48 records duties and verification; physical execution, asset freezing, seizure, or vessel detention requires OCE-47/40 and SCPA.</td></tr><tr><td>T31</td><td>Port-state measure harms an innocent cargo owner</td><td>OCE-35/38 require identity and proportionality; affected cargo interests receive notice, interim relief, appeal, and compensation where due.</td></tr><tr><td>T32</td><td>Major power rejects an MGCC standard</td><td>The standard remains advisory unless validly adopted; coalition pressure or Ethical Trade Zone status does not create global jurisdiction.</td></tr><tr><td>T33</td><td>Thirty percent MPA target is reached</td><td>OCE-50 records the milestone but creates no authority to designate another MPA or declare success despite distributional harm.</td></tr><tr><td>T34</td><td>Ten marine ecosystems receive personhood</td><td>The count is a programme metric only; each later recognition requires its own CERGTA-13 decision.</td></tr><tr><td>T35</td><td>Marine programme closes with open compensation claims</td><td>OCE-49 returns authority and assigns restoration, crew, community, data, correction, and compensation duties without residual command.</td></tr><tr><td>T36</td><td>Port enforcement request includes search and seizure</td><td>OCE-47 records the request; OCE-40, CERGTA-48, and SCPA independently govern acceptance, warrant, execution, custody, termination, and remedy.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>26. Versioning, adoption, and compatibility</h2> <p>A jurisdiction adopts v2.3 only through a controlled instrument identifying the exact version and hash, local authority, powers activated, powers withheld, implementing bodies, transition from v2.2, open cases, current listings and permits, data migration, review date, and withdrawal path.</p> <p>A higher version number or similar title is not automatically compatible. Material changes to rights, Indigenous authority, quotas, licences, port measures, finance, emergency response, investigation, adjudication, or enforcement require a new conformance review.</p> <h2>27. Transition from v2.2</h2> <p>The following v2.2 mechanisms are retired or reclassified:</p> <ul><li>MGCC standards endorsed by a two-thirds state vote → binding only where separately enacted;</li> <li>Coastal BAZ powers assumed by framework → exact host-law delegation through <code>OCE-11</code>;</li> <li>Blue Heart Cultural Council sacred-site protection and advisory veto → affected-nation-controlled advice, confidentiality, reconsideration, and review;</li> <li>Digital Ocean Twin enforcement verification → technical evidence only;</li> <li>AI-adjusted quotas and no-take zones → advisory model followed by competent decisions;</li> <li>automatic blacklisting for acoustic-log tampering → investigation and due-process listing route;</li> <li>500 percent noisy-vessel premium → illustrative tariff option requiring lawful adoption;</li> <li><strong>Absolute Port State Control</strong> → lawful participating port-state measures with actor identity, due process, proportionality, expiry, and humanitarian exceptions;</li> <li>ISA permit → automatic Tier 1 blacklist and port denial → separate legal-position, listing, and port decisions;</li> <li>indefinite full blacklist and two-year removal rule → individualized duration, review, correction, appeal, and delisting;</li> <li>loss of BAZ certification or Ethical Trade Zone access for port non-enforcement → separate lawful or contractual process;</li> <li>automatic 48-hour crisis deployment → CDEE/ECRC mission acceptance;</li> <li>automatic emergency-fund release → separate financial authorization;</li> <li>advisory moratoria treated as universal bans → explicit legal adoption state;</li> <li>five-year default renewal → governing-law renewal or expiry process;</li> <li>legal-personhood timeline → non-activating programme pathway;</li> <li>MPA, seabed, sacred-site, noise, and personhood targets → evaluation metrics only.</li></ul> <h2>28. Closing doctrine</h2> <blockquote><p><strong>The ocean needs rights, stewardship, precaution, restoration, and enforceable protection. Those aims are weakened when a sensor becomes a judge, a tariff becomes a blacklist, a blacklist becomes a closed port, or a closed port becomes collective punishment.</strong></p></blockquote> <blockquote><p><strong>Oceans v2.3 makes marine action stronger by making every authority visible: who observed, who interpreted, who adopted the rule, who set the quota, who listed the actor, who restricted the service, who protected the crew, who adjudicated, who executed, until when, and how the decision can be challenged, corrected, terminated, and repaired.</strong></p></blockquote> <hr> <h1>Controlled cluster-adoption appendix — CEACA/0.1</h1> <p><strong>Patch version:</strong> Oceans & Marine Governance Framework v2.3.1<br> <strong>Substantive base:</strong> Oceans & Marine Governance Framework v2.3<br> <strong>Cluster release:</strong> <code>CEA-CLUSTER/0.1</code><br> <strong>Adoption specification:</strong> <code>CEACA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Authority effect of this appendix:</strong> none; it establishes compatibility, inheritance, routing, and fail-closed rules only.</p> <h2>A.1 Exact compatibility set</h2> <p>This patch participates only in the following controlled document set. A renamed body, later version, compatible-sounding policy, or partial implementation does not silently enter the release. The machine-readable cluster registry freezes the SHA-256 digest of every file.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Exact compatible file</th><th>Cluster role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td><code>commons-ecological-rights-guardianship-title-resource-authority-interface-specification-v0.1.md</code></td><td>controlling authority interface</td></tr><tr><td>EIPHR/0.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-planetary-health-role-separation-matrix-v0.1.md</code></td><td>EIOI–Planetary Health role separation</td></tr><tr><td>EIOI v2.7.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-observation-interpretation-layer-v2.7.1.md</code></td><td>ecological observation, provenance, methods, uncertainty, interpretation, and advisory alerts</td></tr><tr><td>PHG v0.9.1</td><td><code>planetary-health-governance-framework-v0.9.1.md</code></td><td>independent synthesis, participatory audit, cross-domain coherence, and strategic recommendation</td></tr><tr><td>HEARTHSTONE v2.0.1</td><td><code>hearthstone-protocol-v2.0.1.md</code></td><td>voluntary conversion, claims support, title and tenure interfaces, trusts, rematriation, stewardship, and transition</td></tr><tr><td>WATER v5.2.2</td><td><code>water-sanitation-governance-framework-v5.2.2.md</code></td><td>watershed democracy, universal WASH, allocation, utilities, tariffs, guardianship interfaces, and emergency WASH</td></tr><tr><td>BIODIVERSITY v1.1.1</td><td><code>biodiversity-implementation-framework-v1.1.1.md</code></td><td>Indigenous-led stewardship support, restoration programmes, rights-review support, biodiversity finance interfaces, and accountability</td></tr><tr><td>OCEANS v2.3.1</td><td><code>oceans-marine-governance-framework-v2.3.1.md</code></td><td>marine stewardship, quotas and licensing interfaces, port-state measures, crew protection, precaution, and marine emergencies</td></tr><tr><td>SOIL v3.2.2</td><td><code>soil-health-land-use-governance-framework-v3.2.2.md</code></td><td>living-soil protection, regenerative land-use programmes, microbial commons, tenure interfaces, certification, and remediation</td></tr><tr><td>MOS v2.8.2</td><td><code>moral-operating-system-v2.8.2.md</code></td><td>advisory ethical deliberation, rights imagination, plural translation, and structural self-correction</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>A.2 Controlling rules</h2> <ol><li><code>CERGTA/0.1</code> controls the separation of observation, interpretation, rights review, legal recognition, guardianship, affected Indigenous-nation authority, sacred and biocultural designation, claims, title, tenure, stewardship, allocation, permitting, inspection, urgent protection, restrictions, finance, adjudication, restoration, and closure.</li> <li><code>EIPHR/0.1</code> controls the division among EIOI, the Planetary Health Synthesis Office, and the Planetary Health Council. EIOI supplies evidence; PHSO compiles modular synthesis; PHC interprets and recommends. None may create a downstream legal or financial consequence.</li> <li>MOS is advisory only. Its Dynamic Rights Spectrum, philosophical language, developmental translations, structural-failure hypotheses, and guardianship metaphors never create legal status, classify persons, determine credibility, establish Indigenous consent, or activate consequential action.</li> <li>Hearthstone controls the cluster interface for claims, competing claims, title, tenure, trust constitution, voluntary conversion, rematriation, compensation, debt, tenants, workers, registry correction, receivership, succession, and dissolution. Sectoral ecological frameworks may provide evidence or programme support but cannot decide those questions by implication.</li> <li>Water, Biodiversity, Oceans, and Soil retain only the powers separately constituted in applicable law and recorded through the relevant CERGTA route. Framework membership, a council vote, a dashboard value, a target, a pilot, a future date, or a coalition size does not activate authority.</li> <li>Emergency identity and coordination use CDEE and ECRC. Ecological urgency does not create emergency command, and an emergency declaration does not create ecological title, guardianship, permanent restrictions, receivership, or enforcement authority.</li> <li>Search, seizure, restraint, custody, force, cyber action, border action, intelligence use, asset freezing, or analogous coercive execution requires separate SCPA authority. A commons or ecological claim may justify review; it does not authorize a raid.</li> <li>The specific affected Indigenous nation or nations determine their representatives, applicable law, territorial and relational authority, consent process, internal plurality, protected knowledge, and whether to participate. Global or framework-created Indigenous bodies may support or audit but cannot consent or waive rights for another people.</li> <li>Essential minimums for water, sanitation, health, disability support, food, shelter, rescue, crew welfare, and ecological survival remain protected from commercial, tariff, certification, funding, listing, or transition leverage.</li> <li>Every handoff uses the canonical CERGTA states. Receipt, technical ingestion, silence, consultation, or partial use is not acceptance and never creates authority.</li> <li>Every consequential route keeps its own legal basis, decision-maker, affected persons, evidence, access controls, clock, expiry, challenge, correction, remedy, termination, and data-disposition record.</li> <li>Unknown, missing, stale, or incompatible versions fail closed for consequential action. Non-coercive care, emergency life-safety support, accessibility, explanation, and correction should continue wherever lawful and safe.</li></ol> <h2>A.3 Record inheritance and packaging</h2> <p>A local record may implement, extend, split, contain, import, or mark a CERGTA record not applicable. It may not erase the canonical identity, merge distinct powers, weaken an affected-person safeguard, or use a compound package to supply a missing legal basis.</p> <p>The forty-field CERGTA header, access classification, handoff state, independent clocks, correction propagation, remedy, termination, authority return, and data disposition are inherited wherever the substantive base creates or transports a consequential record. Advisory and observation-only records must state their prohibited inferences.</p> <h2>A.4 Registry and compatibility failure</h2> <p>The file <code>commons-ecological-authority-cluster-conformance-registry-v0.1.json</code> is the machine-readable release registry. It is evidence of the exact release set, not an authority record. A hash mismatch, missing component, unsupported local extension, or unresolved version conflict changes the compatibility state to <code>failed_closed</code>.</p> <p>A compatibility failure blocks new consequential cross-framework handoffs. It does not by itself terminate independently valid local law, erase existing rights, interrupt essential services, or authorize emergency or coercive action.</p> <h2>A.5 Closure</h2> <p>A cluster transaction closes only after all accepted handoffs, powers, services, persons, property, guardianship duties, claims, title or tenure effects, financial actions, emergency dependencies, coercive dependencies, corrections, remedies, clocks, credentials, records, and residual risks are resolved or assigned to a named lawful custodian without preserving expired power.</p> <h2>A.6 Editorial patch note</h2> <p>This patch is <strong>editorial only</strong>. No doctrine, power, boundary, record, gate, or test was added, removed, or weakened.</p> <p>Changes:</p> <ol><li><strong>§1 source freeze.</strong> Clarified as a provenance record rather than an operative dependency set, resolving an ambiguity against Appendix A.1. The Oceans v2.2 pin now uses the canonical filename instead of a local download-artifact name (<code>v2.2_2.md</code>); the digest is unchanged and remains valid, as content hashes are name-independent.</li> <li><strong>A.1 compatibility set.</strong> Updated to name the current editions of Water, Soil, and the Moral Operating System, which were editorially revised after this release. The change is editorial in each case, so the correct response is to update the pin rather than treat the mismatch as a substantive conflict.</li></ol> <p>This document required no other change. Its fifty OCE records were already tabular, and every row of its CERGTA crosswalk carries a distinct boundary statement rather than a repeated default.</p>",1);function c(t){var e=r();n(528),a(t,e)}export{c as default};
